const V5_PACK={"revision":"v5-equations-with-each-step","blocks":{"data":{"title":"DATA TYPE + SYMBOL KEY","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Classify a measurement or choose a test.</p><p><b>STEPS</b> 1. Measured on a numerical scale? Continuous: height/BP; discrete: number of tender joints. 2. Categories? Ordered: cancer stage/Apgar; nominal: country/ZIP/event. Numbered categories need not have equal gaps.</p><p><b>SYMBOLS</b> \\(X\\): random measurement; \\(x_i\\): observation; \\(\\bar x\\): sample mean; \\(\\mu\\): population mean; \\(s,\\sigma\\): sample/population SD; \\(s^2,\\sigma^2\\): variances; \\(n\\): sample count; \\(\\nu\\): df; \\(\\alpha\\): significance level; \\(f\\): mass/density; \\(F\\): CDF.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> Height: interval-continuous; ZIP: categorical-nominal.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> A numerical label is not automatically a measured amount.</p>","topics":["classification","start","choose"],"sources":["session2.pdf","hw1.pdf:1"],"location":{"page":1,"column":1,"box":1}},"summary":{"title":"RAW LIST → MEAN, VARIANCE, SD, SEM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A list of measurements; estimate center, spread, or error in the estimated mean.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Heights: \\(65,69,59,64,62,67,68,62,64,65\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\sum x_i=645,\\quad n=10\\]\\[\\bar x=\\frac{\\sum x_i}{n}=\\frac{645}{10}=64.5\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x_i&65&69&59&64&62\\\\x_i-64.5&.5&4.5&-5.5&-.5&-2.5\\\\(x_i-64.5)^2&.25&20.25&30.25&.25&6.25\\\\\\hline x_i&67&68&62&64&65\\\\x_i-64.5&2.5&3.5&-2.5&-.5&.5\\\\(x_i-64.5)^2&6.25&12.25&6.25&.25&.25\\end{array}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2=82.5\\]\\[s^2=\\frac{\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2}{n-1}=\\frac{82.5}{9}=9.16667\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[s=\\sqrt{s^2}=\\sqrt{9.16667}=3.02765\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SEM}=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}=\\frac{3.02765}{\\sqrt{10}}=.957427\\]</div><p>Equivalent squared-sum shortcut:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\sum x_i^2-\\frac{(\\sum x_i)^2}{n}=41685-645^2/10=82.5\\]</div><p>Full-population formulas:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mu=\\frac{\\sum x_i}{N},\\quad\\sigma^2=\\frac{\\sum(x_i-\\mu)^2}{N}\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Estimated population mean: 64.5 inches; individual SD: 3.02765 inches; mean-estimation error (SEM): .957427 inch.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> SD describes individuals; SEM describes sample means. Entire population measured? Use \\(N\\) and divide squared distances by \\(N\\), not \\(n-1\\).</p>","topics":["summary","estimators"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:4","session2.pdf"],"location":{"page":1,"column":1,"box":2}},"percentile":{"title":"SAMPLE PERCENTILE + DATA DISPLAY","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A sorted/raw list and a requested percentile, median, IQR or shape.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Sorted \\(3,7,8,9,14\\); 30th percentile:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r=1+\\frac{p(n-1)}{100}=1+\\frac{30(5-1)}{100}=2.2=I+d\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[x_p=x_{(I)}+d[x_{(I+1)}-x_{(I)}]\\]\\[x_{30}=7+.2(8-7)=7.2\\]</div><p>Median: middle value for odd count; average two middle values for even count. IQR: Q3 minus Q1. Range: minimum to maximum; width: maximum minus minimum.</p></div><p><b>WRITE</b> The 30th sample percentile is 7.2. A roughly straight Normal Q–Q plot supports a Normal shape.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Histogram: bin counts; box: Q1–Q3 with median; whisker rule: 1.5×IQR; violin width: concentration. Use the lecture rank rule, not an n+1 rule.</p>","topics":["percentile","plots"],"sources":["session2.pdf","session5.pdf:16–17"],"location":{"page":1,"column":1,"box":3}},"discrete":{"title":"PROBABILITY TABLE → MOMENTS + CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Separate possible values with a probability attached to each.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[f(x)\\ge0,\\quad\\sum_x f(x)=.55+.15+.10+.20=1\\]\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}x&-1&2&3&7\\\\f(x)&.55&.15&.10&.20\\\\xf(x)&-.55&.30&.30&1.40\\\\x^2f(x)&.55&.60&.90&9.80\\end{array}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mu=E[X]=\\sum_x xf(x)=-.55+.30+.30+1.40=1.45\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X^2]=\\sum_x x^2f(x)=.55+.60+.90+9.80=11.85\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=11.85-1.45^2=9.74750\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sum_x(x-\\mu)^2f(x)\\]\\[E[(X-\\mu)^2]=.55(-2.45)^2+.15(.55)^2\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"6\"></span>\\[\\qquad+.10(1.55)^2+.20(5.55)^2=9.74750\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\sum_{x\\le t}f(x)\\]\\[F(t)=\\begin{cases}0&t<-1\\\\.55&-1\\le t<2\\\\.70&2\\le t<3\\\\.80&3\\le t<7\\\\1&t\\ge7\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(2\\le X<7)=F(7^-)-F(2^-)=.80-.55=.25\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Mean 1.45; variance 9.74750; probability of 2 up to but excluding 7 is .25.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> \\(F(a^-)\\) excludes mass at \\(a\\). \\(P(X>a)=1-F(a)\\); \\(P(X\\ge a)=1-F(a^-)\\). A total of .9 is invalid.</p>","topics":["random","pmf","dcdf","expect","variance"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:1–2","session3.pdf"],"location":{"page":1,"column":2,"box":1}},"loaded":{"title":"LOADED DIE → WEIGHT BY THE STATED MASSES","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A die has unequal probabilities; asks a chance, mean or variance.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Loaded die: \\(f(1)=.20\\), \\(f(2),\\ldots,f(6)=.16\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\sum_xf(x)=.20+5(.16)=1\\]\\[P(X\\le3)=F(3)=\\sum_{x\\le3}f(x)=.20+.16+.16=.52\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X]=\\sum_x xf(x)=1(.20)+(2+3+4+5+6)(.16)\\]\\[=3.4\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X^r]=\\sum_x x^rf(x)\\]\\[E[X^2]=.20+(4+9+16+25+36)(.16)=14.6\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=14.6-3.4^2=3.04\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Loaded-die mean 3.4, variance 3.04.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Counting favorable faces out of six is valid only when all faces are equally likely.</p>","topics":["pmf","expect","variance"],"sources":["session3.pdf:19,24,26"],"location":{"page":1,"column":2,"box":2}},"events":{"title":"SEQUENCE OF EVENTS → UPDATE WHAT REMAINS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Coins/cards or sequential trials; asks the chance of a combined outcome.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Two kings without replacement: after a king, 3 kings remain among 51 cards.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B\\mid A)\\]\\[P(\\text{two kings})=\\frac4{52}\\frac3{51}=.00452489\\]</div><p>Three fair tosses, exactly two heads: HHT, HTH, THH.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P=3/8=.375\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> The chance of two kings is .00452489.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Independent events retain the original second chance. Past heads do not alter the next fair toss.</p>","topics":["independent","random"],"sources":["session3.pdf:13–16"],"location":{"page":1,"column":3,"box":3}},"discrete-sketch":{"title":"DRAW A DISCRETE MASS FUNCTION + CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Sketch a discrete probability/distribution function.</p><ol><li>Mark each allowed x; draw its mass as a vertical stem.</li><li>CDF: \\[F(t)=\\sum_{x\\le t}f(x)\\]Hold each level flat, then jump by the next mass.</li><li>Fill the new/right level; leave the old/left level open.</li></ol><img class=\"v5-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-discrete.svg\" alt=\"Homework discrete masses and step CDF with open left levels and filled new values\"><p><b>WORKED / WRITE</b> At \\(x=2\\), the CDF jumps from .55 to .70. Thus \\(F(2)=.70\\), while \\(P(X<2)=.55\\).</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Only the CDF must never decrease.</p>","topics":["dcdf","pmf"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:2","session3.pdf:23–24"],"location":{"page":1,"column":2,"box":3}},"continuous":{"title":"DENSITY → AREA, CDF, MEAN, VARIANCE, MEDIAN","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A density formula over a continuous interval; probabilities are areas.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Analysis time: \\(f(x)=x/2\\), \\(0\\le x\\le2\\), zero elsewhere.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^2\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_0^2=1\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\int_a^t f(u)\\,du\\]\\[F(t)=\\int_0^t u/2\\,du=t^2/4\\quad(0\\le t\\le2)\\]</div><p>CDF is zero below 0 and one above 2.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X<1)=F(1)=\\frac{1^2}{4}=.25\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X>t)=1-F(t)\\]\\[P(X>1.5)=1-\\frac{1.5^2}{4}=.4375\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X]=\\int_a^b xf(x)\\,dx\\]\\[\\mu=\\int_0^2x(x/2)\\,dx=[x^3/6]_0^2=4/3\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X^r]=\\int_a^b x^rf(x)\\,dx\\quad(r=2)\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^2x^2(x/2)\\,dx=[x^4/8]_0^2=2\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=2-(4/3)^2=2/9\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(q)=.5:\\quad q^2/4=.5\\Rightarrow q=\\sqrt2=1.41421\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Mean time 1.33333 hours; median 1.41421 hours; variance .222222 hours².</p><p><b>TRAP</b> An exact continuous point has probability zero. Density height can exceed one; total area cannot. Median is not necessarily the interval midpoint.</p>","topics":["density","ccdf","cexpect","integrate"],"sources":["session4.pdf","session5.pdf"],"location":{"page":1,"column":3,"box":1}},"piecewise":{"title":"PIECEWISE DENSITY → CARRY EARLIER AREA","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> The density changes its expression at a boundary.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>\\(f(x)=x\\) on 0–1; \\(f(x)=2-x\\) on 1–2.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(1)=\\int_0^1u\\,du=1/2\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\int_a^s f_1(u)\\,du+\\int_s^t f_2(u)\\,du\\quad(t>s)\\]\\[F(t)=\\tfrac12+\\int_1^t(2-u)\\,du\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"3\"></span>\\[=\\tfrac12+[2u-u^2/2]_1^t=2t-t^2/2-1\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\begin{cases}0&t<0\\\\t^2/2&0\\le t\\le1\\\\2t-t^2/2-1&1<t\\le2\\\\1&t>2\\end{cases}\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Both interior formulas give .5 at 1; the final value is 1 at 2.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Do not restart the accumulated probability at zero when the density formula changes.</p>","topics":["ccdf","integrate"],"sources":["session4.pdf"],"location":{"page":1,"column":3,"box":2}},"endpoint":{"title":"UNKNOWN CONSTANT / ENDPOINT → TOTAL AREA ONE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A density contains an unknown normalizing constant or support endpoint.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>\\(f(x)=.1+x\\) for \\(0<x\\le c\\); zero otherwise.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\int_{\\mathrm{support}}f(x)\\,dx=1\\]\\[1=\\int_0^c(.1+x)\\,dx=[.1x+x^2/2]_0^c\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[.1c+c^2/2=1\\Rightarrow c^2+.2c-2=0\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[Ac^2+Bc+D=0:\\quad c=\\frac{-B\\pm\\sqrt{B^2-4AD}}{2A}\\]\\[c=\\frac{-.2+\\sqrt{.2^2-4(1)(-2)}}2=1.31774\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\begin{cases}0&t\\le0\\\\.1t+t^2/2&0<t\\le c\\\\1&t>c\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X>.5)=1-[.1(.5)+.5^2/2]=.825\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(0<X<.6)=.1(.6)+.6^2/2=.24\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X=.5)=0\\]</div><p>For the mean, multiply by x first:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mu=\\int_0^c x(.1+x)\\,dx=[.05x^2+x^3/3]_0^c\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"9\"></span>\\[=.05c^2+c^3/3=.849556\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"10\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[2X+1]=2\\mu+1=2(.849556)+1=2.69911\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> The support ends at 1.31774; mean .849556; chance above .5 is .825.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Keep the unrounded root in subsequent work. A negative root cannot be this upper endpoint.</p>","topics":["density","ccdf","integrate","cexpect"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:3"],"location":{"page":2,"column":1,"box":1}},"uniform":{"title":"UNIFORM → WIDTH DIVIDED BY FULL WIDTH","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A constant density over a fixed range.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[f(x)=\\frac1{b-a}=\\frac1{1-0}=1\\quad(0<x<1)\\]\\[F(t)=\\frac{t-a}{b-a}=\\frac{t-0}{1-0}=t\\quad(0<t<1)\\]\\[P(l<X<u)=\\frac{u-l}{b-a}=u-l\\quad(0\\le l<u\\le1)\\]</div><p>Standard Uniform on 0–1:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X]=\\int_0^1x\\,dx=[x^2/2]_0^1=1/2\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^1x^2\\,dx=[x^3/3]_0^1=1/3\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1/3-(1/2)^2=1/12\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mu=(a+b)/2,\\quad\\sigma^2=(b-a)^2/12\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Standard Uniform mean .5 and variance .0833333.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> CDF is zero before a, a rising line inside, one after b.</p>","topics":["uniform","cexpect"],"sources":["session4.pdf","session5.pdf"],"location":{"page":2,"column":1,"box":2}},"coefficient":{"title":"DENSITY COEFFICIENT","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Unknown multiplier; fixed support.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Analysis-time density on 0–2:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[1=\\int_a^b kg(x)\\,dx=k\\int_a^b g(x)\\,dx\\]\\[1=\\int_0^2 kx\\,dx=k[x^2/2]_0^2=2k\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[k=1/2\\Rightarrow f(x)=x/2\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Density: x/2 on 0–2.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Normalize over the full support.</p>","topics":["density","integrate"],"sources":["session4.pdf:analysis-time density"],"location":{"page":2,"column":1,"box":3}},"continuous-sketch":{"title":"DRAW THE CONTINUOUS PDF + CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Sketch the density/distribution function after solving for the support.</p><ol><li>Mark 0 and \\(c\\approx1.318\\) on x; label the vertical axis f or F.</li><li>\\[f(x)=.1+x\\]PDF: straight line from open \\((0,.1)\\) to filled \\((c,1.418)\\); zero outside. The point \\((0,0)\\) is filled.</li><li>\\[F(x)=.1x+x^2/2\\]CDF: through \\((0,0),(.5,.175),(c,1)\\); bend upward, then remain at one.</li></ol>\\[F(.5)=.1(.5)+(.5)^2/2=.175\\]<img class=\"v5-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-continuous.svg\" alt=\"Homework rising density with open and filled endpoints, and its continuous convex CDF\"><p><b>WRITE</b> CDF is continuous from zero to one. <b>TRAP</b> A density jump need not cause a CDF jump.</p>","topics":["density","ccdf"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:3"],"location":{"page":2,"column":1,"box":4}},"normal":{"title":"NORMAL → LEFT / RIGHT / BETWEEN AREA","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Normal mean and SD plus a measurement cutoff; \\(X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\).</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Glucose: \\(\\mu=95,\\sigma=8.7\\) mg/dL.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_{85}=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}=\\frac{85-95}{8.7}=-1.14943\\approx-1.15\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_{115}=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}=\\frac{115-95}{8.7}=2.29885\\approx2.30\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\Phi(z)=P(Z\\le z)\\]\\[\\Phi(1.15)=.8749,\\quad\\Phi(2.30)=.9893\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X<85)=\\Phi(-1.15)=1-\\Phi(1.15)\\]\\[=1-.8749=.1251\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(l<X<u)=\\Phi(z_u)-\\Phi(z_l)\\]\\[P(85\\le X<115)=.9893-.1251=.8642\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X\\ge115)=1-.9893=.0107\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[.1251+.8642+.0107=1\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_0=(0-95)/8.7=-10.9195\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"9\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X<0)\\approx0,\\quad P(X=95)=0\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> z table: find the complete positive z value in a repeated z/area pair; the adjacent area is cumulative LEFT probability.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> About 86.42% fall between 85 and 115 mg/dL using the table.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> The second Normal parameter is variance; take its square root for SD. Individual: use \\(\\sigma\\); average of n: use SEM at <a class=\"method-reference\" href=\"#v5-sampling\" data-target=\"sampling\">Sample means and SEM</a>.</p>","topics":["normal","normalprob"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:3","session5.pdf"],"location":{"page":2,"column":2,"box":1}},"quantile":{"title":"GIVEN AREA → NORMAL CUTOFF / PERCENTILE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A requested percentile, top-tail fraction, or central range.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Glucose 97.5th percentile / top 2.5%:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[p=.975,\\quad z_p=\\Phi^{-1}(p)=\\Phi^{-1}(.975)=1.96\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[x_p=\\mu+z_p\\sigma=95+1.96(8.7)=112.052\\]</div><p>Lower/upper quartiles:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_{.25}=-.674490,\\quad z_{.75}=.674490\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[Q_1=95-.674490(8.7)=89.1319\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[Q_3=95+.674490(8.7)=100.868\\]</div><p>Middle 95% leaves .025 in each tail:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[p_L=(1-.95)/2=.025,\\quad p_U=1-.025=.975\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[x_L=95-1.96(8.7)=77.948\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[x_U=95+1.96(8.7)=112.052\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Search the z table for .9750 to read 1.96. For a right-tail area, first subtract it from one.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> 97.5% lie below about 112.052 mg/dL; central 95% spans 77.948–112.052.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> A data-list percentile uses the rank rule at <a class=\"method-reference\" href=\"#v5-percentile\" data-target=\"percentile\">Sample percentiles</a>, not inverse Normal.</p>","topics":["normalquant"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:3","session5.pdf:9"],"location":{"page":2,"column":2,"box":2}},"normal-sketch":{"title":"NORMAL PDF / CDF → LABEL MEAN + SCALE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Write or sketch the Normal density/distribution.</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/(2\\sigma^2)}\\]\\[f(x)=\\frac1{8.7\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-95)^2/(2(8.7)^2)}\\]</div><p>PDF: symmetric bell at 95; mark 86.3 and 103.7 (one SD each side).</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^t\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(u-\\mu)^2/(2\\sigma^2)}\\,du\\]\\[F(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^t\\frac1{8.7\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(u-95)^2/(2(8.7)^2)}\\,du\\]</div><p>CDF: rising S through (95,.5), flattening toward 0 and 1. Insert mean 95 and SD 8.7.</p><img class=\"v5-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-normal.svg\" alt=\"Normal glucose bell at 95 and S-shaped CDF through 95 one half\"><p><b>WRITE</b> The integral is sufficient for the CDF. <b>TRAP</b> CDF height is probability below x; PDF height alone is not probability.</p>","topics":["normal","normalprob"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:3","session5.pdf"],"location":{"page":2,"column":2,"box":3}},"r-normal":{"title":"READ pnorm / qnorm + lower.tail","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A supplied Normal command or its result.</p><p><b>STEPS</b> 1. Identify cutoff versus probability input. 2. Read mean, SD. 3. FALSE means RIGHT tail; default TRUE means LEFT tail.</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\texttt{pnorm}:x\\longmapsto F(x)\\]<pre class=\"r-print\">pnorm(85,95,8.7)\n[1] 0.1251903\npnorm(115,95,8.7,lower.tail=FALSE)\n[1] 0.01075671</pre></div><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\texttt{qnorm}:p\\longmapsto x_p\\]<pre class=\"r-print\">qnorm(.975,95,8.7)\n[1] 112.0517\nqnorm(.025,95,8.7,lower.tail=FALSE)\n[1] 112.0517</pre></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Right-tail .025 gives the same cutoff as left-tail .975: about 112.052 mg/dL. <b>TRAP</b> The SD argument is not variance; for a sample mean it must be SEM.</p>","topics":["rcode","rbasics","normalprob","normalquant"],"sources":["session15.pdf:63","hw1.pdf:3–4"],"location":{"page":2,"column":3,"box":4}},"sampling":{"title":"AVERAGE OF n → SAMPLING DISTRIBUTION / SEM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Probability concerns an average. For non-Normal individuals, independent n≥30 is the course guideline for a Normal approximation to the mean.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Impurity: \\(\\mu=4,\\sigma=1.5,n=50\\). Find \\(P(3.5<\\bar X<3.8)\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[\\bar X]=\\mu=4,\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\sigma^2/n\\]\\[\\mathrm{SEM}=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}=\\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{50}}=.212132\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_L=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}=\\frac{3.5-4}{.212132}=-2.35702\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[z_U=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}=\\frac{3.8-4}{.212132}=-.942809\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P=\\Phi(-.942809)-\\Phi(-2.35702)\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"5\"></span>\\[=.172889-.00921106=.163678\\]</div><p>With the supplied rounded table:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P=.1736-.0091=.1645\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Round z to −.94 and −2.36, use symmetry, then subtract. Calculator full-precision result differs slightly from rounded table.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> About 16.4% of such sample averages fall between 3.5 and 3.8 grams.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Normal individuals give Normal averages for any n. CLT makes the average approximately Normal at large n; it does not make the original observations Normal.</p>","topics":["sampling"],"sources":["session10.pdf","session15.pdf:23–29"],"location":{"page":2,"column":3,"box":1}},"linear":{"title":"CHANGED VARIABLE / INDEPENDENT SUM → MEAN + VARIANCE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A constant is added, values are rescaled, or independent random variables are added/subtracted.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Subtract 26 from every glucose measurement:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[aX+b]=a\\mu+b\\]\\[E[X-26]=1(95)-26=69\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\sigma^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X-26)=1^2(8.7)^2=75.69,\\quad\\operatorname{SD}=8.7\\]</div><p>Mean uncertainty shrinks because averaging divides a sum by n:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[aX+bY+c]=a\\mu_X+b\\mu_Y+c\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY+c)=a^2\\sigma_X^2+b^2\\sigma_Y^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}\\left(\\frac{\\sum X_i}{n}\\right)=\\frac{n\\sigma^2}{n^2}=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E[h(X)]=\\sum_x h(x)f(x)\\quad\\text{(discrete)}\\]\\[E[h(X)]=\\int h(x)f(x)\\,dx\\quad\\text{(continuous)}\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> A shift changes the mean but not spread; a sum of independent Normal variables is Normal.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> The sum-of-variances rule requires independence. A negative coefficient still contributes positive variance.</p>","topics":["linear","sampling"],"sources":["session5.pdf","session10.pdf"],"location":{"page":2,"column":3,"box":2}},"precision":{"title":"ESTIMATES + WHICH SPREAD?","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Explain reliability of an estimate, SD versus SEM, or changing sample size.</p><p><b>ESTIMATES</b> Estimate population mean with sample mean and population SD with sample SD. Unbiased means correctly centered over repeated samples. For a Normal population, the sample mean is the minimum-variance mean estimator discussed in class.</p><p>Uncertainty in the sample variance:</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\widehat{\\mathrm{SE}}(s^2)=s^2\\sqrt{\\frac2{n-1}}\\]</div><p>Four times the sample size halves SEM:</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SEM}_{4n}=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{4n}}=\\frac12\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}=\\tfrac12\\mathrm{SEM}_n\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> A larger sample gives a more precise mean estimate without necessarily reducing variation among individuals. <b>TRAP</b> SEM has measurement units; variance has squared units.</p>","topics":["estimators","summary","sampling"],"sources":["session2.pdf","session10.pdf","session15.pdf:30–31"],"location":{"page":2,"column":3,"box":3}},"one-t":{"title":"ONE SAMPLE vs FIXED VALUE → t TEST","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One numerical sample compared with a known/reference population mean.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>LDL: reference 115; student \\(\\bar x=103,s=16,n=16\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0,\\quad H_1:\\mu\\ne\\mu_0\\]\\[H_0:\\mu_{\\mathrm{student}}=115\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SEM}=s/\\sqrt n=16/\\sqrt{16}=4\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\bar x-\\mu_0=103-115=-12\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt n}=\\frac{-12}{4}=-3\\]\\[\\nu=n-1=16-1=15\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|t|=3>2.131\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> t table: df row 15; full TWO-TAILED .05 column → 2.131. Use the magnitude for a two-sided alternative.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, a two-sided one-sample t test supports a different population mean student LDL from 115 mg/dL; the sample mean is lower.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> The reference is a fixed number, not another sample. Do not pool it or give it a sample size.</p>","topics":["onet","hypothesis"],"sources":["hw2.pdf:1","session8.pdf:20–24"],"location":{"page":3,"column":1,"box":1}},"pooled-t":{"title":"TWO INDEPENDENT MEANS → POOLED t","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two independent numerical groups; course common-variance assumption; group sizes may differ.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Cognitive scores: \\((\\bar x_1,s_1,n_1)=(49,8.4,22)\\); \\((\\bar x_2,s_2,n_2)=(57,9.5,27)\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\mu_2,\\quad H_1:\\mu_1\\ne\\mu_2\\]\\[s_1^2=8.4^2=70.56,\\quad s_2^2=9.5^2=90.25\\]\\[\\nu=n_1+n_2-2=22+27-2=47\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[s_p^2=\\frac{(n_1-1)s_1^2+(n_2-1)s_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}\\]\\[s_p^2=\\frac{21(70.56)+26(90.25)}{47}=81.4523\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SE}_{\\Delta}=\\sqrt{s_p^2(1/n_1+1/n_2)}\\]\\[\\mathrm{SE}_{\\Delta}=\\sqrt{81.4523(1/22+1/27)}=2.59213\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x_1-\\bar x_2}{\\mathrm{SE}_{\\Delta}}=\\frac{49-57}{2.59213}=-3.08627\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|t|=3.08627>2.013\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> t table: df 47 lies between rows 46 and 48; .05 two-tail cutoffs 2.013 and 2.011. The statistic exceeds both.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, pooled t supports different population mean cognitive scores; the intervention sample mean is higher.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Do not average variances unless group sizes are equal. A negative t records group order, not a negative p value.</p>","topics":["pooledt","hypothesis"],"sources":["hw2.pdf:1","session8.pdf"],"location":{"page":3,"column":1,"box":2}},"t-output":{"title":"R t OUTPUT → HEADER, df, p","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Supplied t output.</p><p><b>STEPS</b> Read header, df and p; compare p with alpha. Height samples:</p><b class=\"r-label\">Default t.test(freshman, senior)</b><pre class=\"r-print\">\tWelch Two Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 15.611, p-value = 0.0003625</pre><b class=\"r-label\">t.test(..., var.equal = TRUE)</b><pre class=\"r-print\">\tTwo Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 18, p-value = 0.0002594</pre><p><b>READ →</b> t = statistic; df = reference distribution; p = decision. Both p values are below .05.</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[p<\\alpha\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]\\[.0003625<.05;\\quad .0002594<.05\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> Population mean heights differ at .05; the senior sample mean is higher. <b>TRAP</b> Use the printed Welch df; do not replace it with pooled df. Hand method: <a class=\"method-reference\" href=\"#v5-pooled-t\" data-target=\"pooled-t\">Pooled t test</a>.</p>","topics":["rcode","pooledt"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:4","session9.pdf:15","session15.pdf:63"],"location":{"page":3,"column":1,"box":3}},"anova":{"title":"SEVERAL EQUAL-SIZE MEANS → BALANCED ANOVA","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Three or more numerical groups, same number n in each; means and SDs supplied.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Tumors: means \\(1188,1412,1363\\); SDs \\(189,217,191\\); \\(m=3,n=12\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\cdots=\\mu_m;\\quad H_1:\\text{not all equal}\\]\\[s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=\\frac{\\sum_i s_i^2}{m}\\]\\[s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=(189^2+217^2+191^2)/3=39763.7\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=\\frac{\\sum_i\\bar x_i}{m}=\\frac{1188+1412+1363}{3}=1321\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[(\\bar x_i-1321)=(-133,91,42)\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\sum_i(\\bar x_i-1321)^2\\]\\[=17689+8281+1764=27734\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{\\sum_i(\\bar x_i-\\bar{\\bar x})^2}{m-1}=\\frac{27734}{2}=13867\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[s_{\\mathrm{bet}}^2=n s_{\\bar x}^2=12(13867)=166404\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F=\\frac{s_{\\mathrm{bet}}^2}{s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2}=\\frac{166404}{39763.7}=4.18483\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\nu_n=m-1=2,\\quad\\nu_d=m(n-1)=3(11)=33\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> F table: numerator df 2 across top; denominator df 33 down side. At upper-tail .05, rows 32 and 34 give 3.29 and 3.28. F exceeds both → reject.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, ANOVA supports a difference in population mean tumor size among dose groups. It does not identify the differing pair.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Use variance, not SD, in the ratio. This balanced formula needs equal group sizes; means/SDs are given for the exam.</p>","topics":["anova"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:5","session15.pdf:33–35","midterm-review-transcript.txt"],"location":{"page":3,"column":2,"box":1}},"conditions":{"title":"MEAN-TEST CONDITIONS + DECISION LANGUAGE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> State assumptions, explain a result, or choose the correct tail.</p><p><b>STEPS</b> Numerical interval outcome; independent random observations; Normal population(s). Pooled t/ANOVA also assume common population variance. Balanced ANOVA has equal group sizes.</p><p><b>WORKED</b> Student HR by pet preference: cat means 74.2, dog 76.8; SDs 7.3, 9.1; five students per group.</p>\\[s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=(7.3^2+9.1^2)/2=68.05\\]\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=(74.2+76.8)/2=75.5\\]\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{(-1.3)^2+1.3^2}{2-1}=3.38\\]\\[s_{\\mathrm{bet}}^2=5(3.38)=16.9,\\quad(\\nu_n,\\nu_d)=(1,8)\\]<div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F=s_{\\mathrm{bet}}^2/s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=16.9/68.05=.248347<5.32\\]\\[F\\text{ or }\\chi^2:\\ \\text{statistic}>\\text{upper cutoff}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject}\\]\\[\\text{two-sided t: }|t|>t_{\\mathrm{crit}}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject}\\]</div><p><b>TABLE</b> F df (1,8), upper .05: 5.32. <b>WRITE</b> A population mean HR difference was not established. <b>TRAP</b> A p value is an extreme-result probability under the null, not the probability that the null is true; not rejecting does not prove equality.</p>","topics":["hypothesis","distributions"],"sources":["session7.pdf:8–14","session8.pdf","session15.pdf"],"location":{"page":3,"column":2,"box":2}},"aov-output":{"title":"REAL summary(aov) → WHAT EACH COLUMN ANSWERS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Supplied ANOVA output.</p><pre class=\"r-print aov-print\">            Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(&gt;F)  \nGroup        2  779.4   389.7   5.832 0.0106 *\nResiduals   19 1269.5    66.8                 \n---\nSignif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1</pre><p><b>READ →</b> Group Df 2 = numerator df; Residuals Df 19 = denominator df and pairwise t df. <b>Mean Sq 66.8</b> = within variance; <b>F value 5.832</b> = statistic; <b>Pr(&gt;F) .0106</b> = p value.</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F=\\frac{\\mathrm{MS}_{\\mathrm{Group}}}{\\mathrm{MS}_{\\mathrm{Residual}}}=5.832\\quad\\text{(unrounded inputs)}\\]</div><p><b>STEPS</b> Compare p with alpha. At .05 reject; at .01 do not reject. For follow-ups, use residual variance and residual df with the actual pair’s sample sizes.</p><p>If only residual standard error 8.174069 is printed, square it:</p>\\[s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=(8.174069)^2=66.8154\\approx66.8\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, at least one population mean exhaled nitric-oxide level differs. <b>TRAP</b> Residual standard error is an SD, not a variance; a star is not the numerical p value.</p>","topics":["rcode","anova","posthoc"],"sources":["session7.pdf:37,39","session15.pdf:63"],"location":{"page":3,"column":1,"box":4,"span":2}},"bonferroni":{"title":"WHICH PAIRS? → BONFERRONI","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Compare a named family of mean pairs at a familywise significance level.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Filgrastim means \\(3.7,4.6,5.2\\); \\(n=15\\) each; given \\(s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2=2.5267,\\nu=42,k=3\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[k=\\begin{cases}m(m-1)/2&\\text{all pairs}\\\\m-1&\\text{vs control}\\end{cases}\\]\\[\\mathrm{SE}_{ij}=\\sqrt{s_{\\mathrm{wit}}^2(1/n_i+1/n_j)}\\]\\[\\mathrm{SE}=\\sqrt{2(2.5267)/15}=.580425\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|t_{13}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_1-\\bar x_3|}{\\mathrm{SE}_{13}}=\\frac{1.5}{.580425}=2.58431\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|t_{12}|=(4.6-3.7)/.580425=1.55059\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|t_{23}|=(5.2-4.6)/.580425=1.03373\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\alpha_{\\mathrm{pair}}=\\alpha_T/k=.05/3=.0166667\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[t_{\\mathrm{crit}}=2.49367;\\quad2.58431>2.49367\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> t row 42, TWO-TAIL level .0166667. If absent, TI-84 invT uses LEFT area 1−.05/(2×3) and df 42. The table uses .0166667; only invT uses the left-area conversion.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> With familywise .05 Bonferroni control, low and high dose population means differ; other pair differences were not established.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Use ANOVA residual df and within variance, not two-group pooled df.</p>","topics":["bonferroni","posthoc"],"sources":["hw2.pdf:1–2","session9.pdf"],"location":{"page":3,"column":3,"box":1}},"holm":{"title":"MULTIPLE PAIRS → HOLM: STOP AT FIRST FAILURE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Holm adjustment; supplied t/chi-square statistics or unadjusted p values.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Five t comparisons; \\(\\nu=46,\\alpha_T=.05\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\alpha_T}{k-j+1},\\quad j=1,\\ldots,k\\]\\[\\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}\\text{pair}&|t|&\\alpha_j&t_{\\mathrm{crit}}\\\\1:6&3.3&.0100&2.687\\\\1:3&2.9&.0125&2.59973\\\\1:5&2.3&.0166667&2.48469\\\\1:4&2.2&.0250&\\\\1:2&2.1&.0500&\\end{array}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[3.3>2.687:\\ \\text{pass};\\quad2.9>2.59973:\\ \\text{pass}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[2.3<2.48469:\\ \\text{STOP}\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> t df row 46; each column is the current full TWO-TAILED alpha. Missing .0125/.0166667 columns: invT left area 1−alpha_j/2.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At familywise .05, G1 differs from G6 and G3; differences from G5, G4 and G2 were not established.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Order p smallest first, or absolute t largest first when df match. Stop at the first failure; remaining comparisons are not significant.</p>","topics":["holm","posthoc"],"sources":["session9.pdf:16","session15.pdf:45"],"location":{"page":3,"column":3,"box":2}},"tables":{"title":"READ THE SUPPLIED TABLES WITHOUT DOUBLING TAILS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A critical value, p-value range, or missing table entry.</p><ol><li><b>z table:</b> find z and its neighboring cumulative LEFT area. Negative z: use symmetry.</li><li><b>t table:</b> row = df; column = total TWO-TAILED alpha. A .05 test uses .05, not .025.</li><li><b>F table:</b> numerator df along top, denominator df down side; use the requested UPPER tail.</li><li><b>Chi-square:</b> df down side; upper-tail probability across top. Ordinary chi-square tables are interchangeable for the same df/tail.</li></ol><p><b>WORKED</b> t=2.58630, df 4:</p>\\[t_{.10,4}=2.132<2.58630<2.776=t_{.05,4}\\]\\[.05<p_{\\mathrm{two-sided}}<.10\\]<p><b>Missing df?</b> Read both neighboring rows. Above both cutoffs → reject; below both → do not reject; between them → an exact cutoff or supplied p is needed.</p><p><b>TI-84</b> Positive t cutoff: invT(1−alpha/2,df). Two-sided p: 2×tcdf(|t|,1E99,df). Normal area: normalcdf(lower,upper,mean,SD); cutoff: invNorm(left area,mean,SD).</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Halve alpha for the invT left-area conversion, not a second time for the supplied two-tailed table.</p>","topics":["distributions","rcode"],"sources":["tables/t_table.pdf","tables/F_table.pdf","tables/Chi_Squared_Table2.pdf","tables/z_table_NormalDist.pdf","session15.pdf:2","hw2.pdf:2"],"location":{"page":3,"column":3,"box":3}},"binary":{"title":"ONE EVENT PROPORTION → ESTIMATE + SE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One group with event/no-event outcomes; estimate the event chance and uncertainty.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Death in 8 of 61 patients:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\hat p=m/n=8/61=.131148\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}(X)=\\hat p(1-\\hat p)=.113948\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\widehat{\\operatorname{SD}}(X)=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}=\\sqrt{.113948}=.337562\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\widehat{\\operatorname{SE}}(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{.113948}{61}}=.0432203\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Estimated death risk .131148; standard error of the estimated proportion .0432203.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Individual binary SD and SE of the estimated proportion answer different questions. Name the same event in every group.</p>","topics":["binary"],"sources":["session11.pdf:3–11"],"location":{"page":4,"column":1,"box":1}},"z-yates":{"title":"TWO PROPORTIONS → YATES z","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two groups; each person has event/no event. Same event definition in both groups.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Placebo 18/25 events; aspirin 6/19:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:p_1=p_2,\\quad H_1:p_1\\ne p_2\\]\\[\\hat p_i=m_i/n_i:\\quad\\hat p_1=18/25=.72\\]\\[\\hat p_2=6/19=.315789,\\quad\\hat p=\\frac{m_1+m_2}{n_1+n_2}=\\frac{24}{44}=.545455\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SE}_0=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)(1/n_1+1/n_2)}\\]\\[\\mathrm{SE}_0=\\sqrt{.545455(.454545)(1/25+1/19)}=.151547\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[c_Y=\\tfrac12(1/n_1+1/n_2)\\]\\[c_Y=.5(1/25+1/19)=.0463158\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[D_Y=\\max(0,|\\hat p_1-\\hat p_2|-c_Y)\\]\\[D_Y=|.72-.315789|-.0463158=.357895\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{D_Y}{\\mathrm{SE}_0}=\\frac{.357895}{.151547}=2.36161>1.960\\]</div><p>Rounded-proportion format (dioxin): .565, .455; groups 186, 220; exactly 205 female births total.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\hat p=205/406=.504926\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{SE}_0=\\sqrt{.504926(.495074)(1/186+1/220)}\\]\\[=.0498017\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[c_Y=.5(1/186+1/220)=.00496090\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"9\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{.565-.455-.00496090}{.0498017}=2.10915>1.960\\]</div><p>Female-birth proportions differ at .05.</p></div><p><b>TABLE</b> t table infinity-df row, full .05 two-tail column → 1.960. Reject.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, corrected two-proportion z supports different population thrombus rates; aspirin’s observed rate is lower.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Always retain Yates for the course’s 2×2 comparisons. If proportions are rounded but an exact total event count is supplied, pool that count.</p>","topics":["zprop","yates"],"sources":["session11.pdf:14–20","hw2.pdf:2"],"location":{"page":4,"column":1,"box":2}},"chi2":{"title":"2×2 COUNTS → YATES CHI-SQUARE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two rows × two outcome columns; asks whether population proportions differ.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[O=\\begin{pmatrix}18&7\\\\6&13\\end{pmatrix},\\quad N=44\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E_{ij}=R_iC_j/N\\]\\[E=\\begin{pmatrix}25(24)/44&25(20)/44\\\\19(24)/44&19(20)/44\\end{pmatrix}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[=\\begin{pmatrix}13.6364&11.3636\\\\10.3636&8.63636\\end{pmatrix}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\chi_Y^2=\\sum_{ij}\\frac{\\max(0,|O_{ij}-E_{ij}|-.5)^2}{E_{ij}}\\]\\[c_{11}=\\frac{(|18-13.6364|-.5)^2}{13.6364}=1.09470\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\chi_Y^2=1.09470+1.31364+1.44039+1.72847\\]\\[=5.57719,\\quad\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)=1\\]</div><pre class=\"r-print\">\tPearson&#x27;s Chi-squared test with Yates&#x27; continuity correction\nX-squared = 5.5772, df = 1, p-value = 0.0182</pre><p><b>READ →</b> The R header explicitly includes Yates. X-squared is the statistic; p-value is the upper-tail probability.</p></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 1, upper-tail .05 → 3.841. For a p range: 5.024 (.025) < 5.57719 < 6.635 (.01), so .01<p<.025.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, corrected chi-square supports different thrombus proportions for placebo and aspirin.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Use counts, not percentages. Corrected z squared equals this corrected chi-square.</p>","topics":["chi","yates","contingency","rcode"],"sources":["session12.pdf:3–11","session11.pdf:19–20"],"location":{"page":4,"column":1,"box":3}},"large-chi":{"title":"LARGER TABLE → ORDINARY CHI-SQUARE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> More than two rows or columns; compare population outcome distributions across groups.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:\\text{equal outcome proportions across groups}\\]\\[O=\\begin{pmatrix}36&131\\\\41&132\\\\75&104\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R=(167,173,179)\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[C=(152,367),\\quad N=519\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E_{11}=\\frac{R_1C_1}{N}=\\frac{167(152)}{519}=48.9094\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[E=\\begin{pmatrix}48.9094&118.091\\\\50.6667&122.333\\\\52.4239&126.576\\end{pmatrix}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[c_{11}=(36-48.9094)^2/48.9094=3.40739\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\chi^2=\\sum_{ij}(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2/E_{ij}\\]\\[\\chi^2=3.40739+1.41124+1.84430+.763851\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"7\"></span>\\[\\qquad+9.72230+4.02667=21.1757,\\quad\\nu=2\\]\\[\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)=(3-1)(2-1)=2\\]</div><pre class=\"r-print\">\tPearson&#x27;s Chi-squared test\nX-squared = 21.176, df = 2, p-value = 2.522e-05</pre><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\texttt{2.522e-05}=.00002522\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 2, upper .05 → 5.991. Reject.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, population outcome distributions differ among the three treatments.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> No Yates for this 3×2 table. Expected counts may be fractional. Follow-up pairs each need a new 2×2 table and new margins.</p>","topics":["contingency","chi","rcode"],"sources":["session12.pdf:13–16"],"location":{"page":4,"column":2,"box":1}},"gof":{"title":"ONE COUNT LIST vs TARGET PROPORTIONS → GOF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One observed category distribution compared with specified expected percentages/probabilities.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Observed \\((255,125,90)\\), \\(N=470\\); targets \\((.60,.25,.15)\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[H_0:p_i=p_{i0}\\ \\text{for all }i\\]\\[E_i=Np_{i0}:\\quad E=(470(.60),470(.25),470(.15))\\]\\[=(282,117.5,70.5)\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\chi^2=\\sum_i(O_i-E_i)^2/E_i\\]\\[\\chi^2=\\frac{(255-282)^2}{282}+\\frac{(125-117.5)^2}{117.5}+\\frac{(90-70.5)^2}{70.5}\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"3\"></span>\\[=2.58511+.478723+5.39362=8.45745,\\quad\\nu=2\\]\\[\\nu=k-1=3-1=2\\]</div><pre class=\"r-print\">\tChi-squared test for given probabilities\nX-squared = 8.4574, df = 2, p-value = 0.01457</pre></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 2, upper .05 → 5.991. Reject.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, the population exercise-category distribution differs from the stated earlier pattern.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Use the specified target probabilities, not the current observed proportions. No 2×2 Yates correction for a one-list goodness-of-fit test.</p>","topics":["gof","rcode"],"sources":["session12.pdf:24–27"],"location":{"page":4,"column":2,"box":2}},"chi-holm":{"title":"SUPPLIED PAIRWISE CHI-SQUARE → CONTROL FAMILY","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Printed pairwise test statistics; asks which treatments differ from control.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Control comparisons: G1 9.4944; G2 4.6895; G3 18.234, each df 1. Six pairs were printed, but only three belong to this requested family.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\alpha_j=\\alpha_T/(k-j+1)\\]\\[k=3,\\quad\\alpha_j=.05/3,\\ .05/2,\\ .05\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[18.234>6.635>\\chi^2_{.0166667,1}:\\ \\text{pass}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[9.4944>\\chi^2_{.025,1}=5.024:\\ \\text{pass}\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[4.6895>\\chi^2_{.05,1}=3.841:\\ \\text{pass}\\]</div></div><p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 1. The .0166667 column is absent, but exceeding the stricter .01 cutoff 6.635 already proves the first pass.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> At familywise .05, G1, G2 and G3 each differ from control in the population outcome proportion.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Do not count unrequested treatment-versus-treatment pairs in k. Keep the named outcome and treatment labels in the answer.</p>","topics":["holm","chi","rcode"],"sources":["hw2.pdf:3–4"],"location":{"page":4,"column":2,"box":3}},"risk":{"title":"PROSPECTIVE RISKS → RR, ARR, NNT / NNH","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Treatment/exposure groups followed for an outcome; asks the size of a risk difference or number treated.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Aspirin 6/19 thrombi; placebo 18/25:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r_T=6/19=.315789,\\quad r_C=18/25=.72\\]\\[\\mathrm{RR}=r_T/r_C=.315789/.72=.438596\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{ARR}=r_C-r_T=.72-.315789=.404211\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{NNT}=1/\\mathrm{ARR}=1/.404211=2.47396\\to3\\text{ people}\\]</div><p>Low-base-rate benefit: deaths 10 to 8 per thousand.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r_C=.010,\\quad r_T=.008,\\quad\\mathrm{RR}=.8\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{RRR}=1-\\mathrm{RR}=1-.8=.2\\]\\[\\mathrm{ARR}=.010-.008=.002,\\quad\\mathrm{NNT}=1/.002=500\\]</div><p>If NNH=20, harms per one additional avoided death:</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{NNH}=1/(r_T-r_C)\\quad\\text{when treatment raises harm}\\]\\[\\mathrm{NNT}/\\mathrm{NNH}=500/20=25\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Aspirin thrombus risk is .438596 times placebo’s; absolute reduction is 40.4211 percentage points; about three treated per additional avoided event.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Risk uses events/total; odds use events/non-events. A 20% relative reduction is not a twenty-percentage-point absolute reduction. Use decimal risks in reciprocals.</p>","topics":["risk","nnt"],"sources":["session13.pdf","session11.pdf:19–20"],"location":{"page":4,"column":3,"box":1}},"odds":{"title":"CASES CHOSEN BY DISEASE → ODDS RATIO","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> People selected as cases/controls by existing outcome, then earlier exposure assessed.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Cases: 50 exposed, 14 unexposed; controls: 43 exposed, 35 unexposed.</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\begin{array}{c|cc}&\\text{exposed}&\\text{unexposed}\\\\\\text{cases}&a&b\\\\\\text{controls}&c&d\\end{array}\\]\\[\\mathrm{OR}=\\frac{a/b}{c/d}=\\frac{ad}{bc}=\\frac{50/14}{43/35}\\]\\[=\\frac{50(35)}{14(43)}=2.90698\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Odds of secondhand-smoke exposure are about 2.90698 times as high among cases as controls.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Case-control sampling does not supply population disease-risk denominators for RR. Reversing one comparison takes the reciprocal. RR/OR=1 means no association.</p>","topics":["odds","risk"],"sources":["session13.pdf:12–16"],"location":{"page":4,"column":3,"box":2}},"design":{"title":"STUDY DESIGN → WHAT WAS ASSIGNED / FOLLOWED?","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Identify study design, masking, or choose risk versus odds.</p><ol><li><b>Randomized trial:</b> assign treatment, then follow outcomes. <b>Cohort:</b> follow exposure groups without random assignment; both support event risks/RR.</li><li><b>Case-control:</b> select by outcome, assess earlier exposure; use OR.</li><li><b>Parallel:</b> different people in treatments. <b>Crossover:</b> same people receive both in different periods; stable disease and no carryover required.</li></ol><p><b>Masking:</b> subject; treatment/evaluation team; statistical team. Each hides assignment from a possible source of expectation bias. A sham controls procedure experience/expectation.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> State what was assigned, how people were selected, and which outcome was followed. <b>TRAP</b> Association alone does not establish causation; volunteers need not represent every patient.</p>","topics":["trials"],"sources":["session10.pdf:23–31","session13.pdf:2–3,12"],"location":{"page":4,"column":3,"box":3}},"folate-risk":{"title":"FOLATE: RISK RATIOS FROM COUNTS","html":"<p><b>GIVEN</b> NTD is the event. Folate: 4 with NTD, 471 without; no folate: 27 with NTD, 498 without.</p><p><b>1. TOTALS</b> Add both outcomes within each group:</p>\\[n_F=471+4=475,\\quad n_{NF}=498+27=525\\]<p><b>2. RISKS</b> Divide events by everyone in that group:</p>\\[r_F=\\frac4{475}=.00842105,\\quad r_{NF}=\\frac{27}{525}=.0514286\\]<p><b>3. RATIO</b> For risk without folate relative to risk with folate:</p>\\[\\mathrm{RR}=\\frac{r_{NF}}{r_F}=\\frac{27/525}{4/475}=6.10714\\]<p><b>RESULT</b> The no-folate group’s NTD risk was about 6.11 times the folate group’s risk.</p><p>Swapping the groups takes the reciprocal:</p>\\[\\mathrm{RR}_{F:NF}=1/\\mathrm{RR}_{NF:F}=.163743\\]","topics":["risk","odds"],"sources":["hw2.pdf:2","session13.pdf:4–10"],"location":{"page":4,"column":3,"box":4}},"quartile-steps":{"title":"QUARTILES + IQR: LOCATE, INTERPOLATE, SUBTRACT","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A data list; asks for quartiles, middle spread or a box plot.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Sorted heights: \\(59,62,62,64,64,65,65,67,68,69\\); \\(n=10\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r=1+\\frac{p(n-1)}{100}:\\quad r_{25}=1+.25(9)=3.25\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[Q_1=x_{(3)}+.25[x_{(4)}-x_{(3)}]\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"3\"></span>\\[=62+.25(64-62)=62.5\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r_{50}=1+.50(9)=5.5\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\text{median}=64+.5(65-64)=64.5\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"6\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[r_{75}=1+.75(9)=7.75\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"7\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[Q_3=65+.75(67-65)=66.5\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"8\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[\\mathrm{IQR}=Q_3-Q_1=66.5-62.5=4\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Middle half of the heights: 62.5–66.5 inches; median 64.5 inches.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> Use the fractional rank to move between two sorted observations; do not round it to the nearest rank.</p>","topics":["percentile","plots"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:4","session2.pdf","session15.pdf:7"],"location":{"page":1,"column":1,"box":4}},"cdf-endpoints":{"title":"DISCRETE CDF: INCLUDE OR EXCLUDE THE ENDPOINT","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A discrete probability with a strict or inclusive inequality.</p><div class=\"worked-sequence\"><p>Use the probability table above. \\(F(a^-)\\) is the total just before \\(a\\).</p><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"1\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[F(2^-)=.55,\\quad F(2)=.55+.15=.70\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"2\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a):\\quad P(X>2)=1-.70=.30\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"3\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(X\\ge a)=1-F(a^-):\\quad P(X\\ge2)=1-.55=.45\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"4\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(a<X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a)\\]\\[P(2<X\\le7)=F(7)-F(2)=1-.70=.30\\]</div><div class=\"v5-step\" data-origin=\"5\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(-1<X<3)=F(3^-)-F(-1)\\]<span class=\"continuation-origin\" data-origin=\"6\"></span>\\[=.70-.55=.15\\]</div></div><p><b>WRITE</b> Including 2 adds its mass .15: the right-tail chance changes from .30 to .45.</p><p><b>TRAP</b> A discrete point can have positive probability. The continuous rule that a single point has zero probability does not apply.</p>","topics":["dcdf","pmf"],"sources":["hw1.pdf:2","session3.pdf","session4.pdf:3–4"],"location":{"page":1,"column":2,"box":4}},"integral-steps":{"title":"INTEGRALS: SUBSTITUTE BOTH BOUNDS","html":"<p>Analysis time: \\(f(x)=x/2\\) on 0–2. An antiderivative \\(G\\) is a function whose derivative is \\(f\\).</p><p><b>AREA</b> Integrate the density; insert upper and lower bounds:</p><div class=\"v5-step\"><span class=\"step-number\"></span>\\[P(l<X<u)=F(u)-F(l)=\\int_l^u f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[=G(u)-G(l)\\]\\[P(1.5<X\\le2)=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_{1.5}^{2}\\]\\[=\\frac{2^2}{4}-\\frac{1.5^2}{4}=1-.5625=.4375\\]</div><p><b>MEAN</b> Multiply the density by x before integrating:</p>\\[E[X]=\\int_0^2x\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{6}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\frac{2^3}{6}-\\frac{0^3}{6}=\\frac86=\\frac43\\]","topics":["integrate","density","cexpect"],"sources":["session4.pdf:20–21"],"location":{"page":1,"column":3,"box":4}}},"sheets":[{"columns":[["data","summary","percentile","quartile-steps"],["discrete","loaded","discrete-sketch","cdf-endpoints"],["continuous","piecewise","events","integral-steps"]]},{"columns":[["endpoint","uniform","coefficient","continuous-sketch"],["normal","quantile","normal-sketch"],["sampling","linear","precision","r-normal"]]},{"columns":[["one-t","pooled-t","t-output"],["anova","conditions"],["bonferroni","holm","tables"]],"bottom":[{"column":1,"span":2,"keys":["aov-output"]}]},{"columns":[["binary","z-yates","chi2"],["large-chi","gof","chi-holm"],["risk","odds","design","folate-risk"]]}],"margins":0.3,"minimumBodyPt":7};
